# Graph Switching

Graph Switching is a collection of small C programs for enumerating graphs
obtained by applying Godsil--McKay (GM) or Wang--Qiu--Hu (WQH) switching once
to a regular graph. The programs were written for experiments with strongly
regular graphs.

> **Development notice:** Unlike the original code, `graphswitching` will be
> mostly vibe coded. Use it at your own risk.

## Programs

| Program | Switching operation | Configuration |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `graphswitching` | GM by default; WQH when requested | Runtime options; recommended interface |
| `gen_all_srgs` | GM with parts of size `4,n-4` | Set `VTS` in the source |
| `gen_all_srgs_64vts` | Bit-packed GM with parts of size `4,n-4` | Set `VTS`; limited by the width of `INTT` |
| `gen_all_srgs_wqh6` | WQH with parts of size `3,3,n-6` | Set `VTS` in the source |
| `gen_all_srgs_wqh_generic` | WQH with parts of size `p,p,n-2p` | Original standalone implementation |

`graphswitching` supports at most 448 vertices. Its WQH method has a maximum
part size of 8.

## Quick start

Build all five programs:

```sh
make
```

Run the default GM method. The number of vertices is inferred from the
matrix:

```sh
./graphswitching < adjacency-matrix.txt \
    > switched-matrices.txt
```

Use `./graphswitching --help` to see every option.

See `INSTALL` for compiler requirements, installation options, and details
about configuring the specialized programs.

## Input

Each program reads an adjacency matrix from standard input. It consumes
exactly `n * n` occurrences of `0` or `1`; all other characters, including
spaces and newlines, are ignored. For example, a matrix may be written with
one row per line.

The programs expect an adjacency matrix for a regular, simple, undirected
graph. They do not comprehensively validate those properties.

For the three specialized programs, `n` is the compile-time `VTS` value in
the corresponding source file. `gen_all_srgs_wqh_generic` takes `n` and `p`
as positional arguments.

```text
gen_all_srgs_wqh_generic n p
```

`graphswitching` infers `n` by counting the binary entries of a square input
matrix. It also accepts a leading `n=<number of vertices>` line. Use
`--vertices N` to specify the order explicitly.

## Command-line interface

`graphswitching` uses GM switching by default. All configuration is optional:

```text
Usage: graphswitching [OPTION]...

  -m METHOD, --method=METHOD
  -n N, --vertices=N
  -p P, --part-size=P
  -i FILE, --input=FILE
  -o FILE, --output=FILE
  -h, --help
  -V, --version
```

`--help` documents every option and accepted switching method, the defaults,
input and output formats, limits, examples, and exit statuses. Both
`--option=value` and `--option value` forms are accepted for options with
values; short options accept both `-x value` and `-xvalue`.

For example, explicitly request WQH switching with parts of size 3:

```sh
graphswitching --method wqh --part-size 3 \
    --input adjacency-matrix.txt --output switched-matrices.txt
```

WQH switching with parts of size `2,2,n-4` corresponds to four-vertex GM
switching. The default uses the dedicated enumeration from `gen_all_srgs`,
which considers each four-vertex switching set directly. Use
`--method wqh` only when the WQH partition enumerator is specifically
desired.

The project version is recorded in `VERSION` and is available at run time:

```sh
graphswitching --version
```

## Output

Output is written to standard output. The first line is:

```text
n=<number of vertices>
```

It is followed by the adjacency matrices found by one switching step, with
a blank line after each matrix. The search can produce isomorphic graphs;
the programs do not remove duplicates up to isomorphism.

The matrix output is compatible with the adjacency-matrix tools from
[nauty and Traces](https://pallini.di.uniroma1.it/). For example, when the
prefixed gtools names are installed:

```sh
./graphswitching < adjacency-matrix.txt \
    | nauty-amtog -q \
    | nauty-labelg -gqt \
    | LC_ALL=C sort -u > switched.g6
```

nauty and Traces are optional and are not needed to build or run the
switching programs themselves.

## Project layout

- `include/graphswitching.h` contains the public C interface.
- `src/graphswitching.c` contains the reusable GM and WQH implementations.
- `tools/graphswitching.c` contains the command-line interface and its
  buffered output setup.
- `code/` contains the unchanged original standalone programs.
- `tests/` contains regular-graph inputs used to compare the new tool with
  the original, plus a generator/validator for the algebraic fixtures.
- `benchmarks/` contains reproducible end-to-end timing cases.

The command-line parsing and matrix output are kept separate from the search
implementation so other input and output formats can be added without
restructuring the switching algorithms.

## Specialized builds

The specialized source files hard-code the graph order. Edit the relevant
`#define VTS` and rebuild that target:

```sh
make clean
make gen_all_srgs
```

`gen_all_srgs_64vts` stores each adjacency row in one `INTT`; consequently,
`VTS` must not exceed the number of bits in that type. See the comments in
`code/gen_all_srgs_64vts.c` before changing `VTS` or `INTT`.

## Performance

The programs enumerate candidate switching sets combinatorially, so running
time and output size can grow quickly with the number of vertices and the
part size. `graphswitching` writes complete rows and uses a larger standard
output buffer to reduce output overhead. The default build also uses
optimization and loop-unrolling flags. Override them when needed, for
example:

```sh
make clean
make CFLAGS="-O2"
```

## Benchmarks

The `benchmarks/` directory contains reproducible end-to-end benchmarks.
They run a switching program, convert its matrices with nauty, canonically
label the graphs, remove duplicates, verify the number of isomorphism
classes, and report wall, user, and system time.

Run the quick Sp(6,2) comparison of legacy GM, the default
`graphswitching` GM method, and explicit WQH with:

```sh
make benchmark
```

Run the larger tuple-enumeration cases or repeat selected cases:

```sh
make benchmark BENCHMARK_SUITE=full BENCHMARK_RUNS=3
make benchmark BENCHMARK_CASES="sp6-wqh-p2 paley73-wqh-p3"
```

The runner automatically recognizes both the unprefixed nauty program names
(`amtog`, `labelg`) and the names used by some Linux distributions
(`nauty-amtog`, `nauty-labelg`). See
[`benchmarks/README.md`](benchmarks/README.md) for explicit overrides and a
description of every case.

## Background

Examples, data sets, and more context about the strongly regular graph
computations are available on
[Ferdinand Ihringer's SRG page](https://ihringer.org/srgs.php).

